var additionalData = [
["history.html","Revision History",
"\t\t\nRevision History\nVersion 3.0 (2022-10-25)\n \tLocation of Changes \tChanges\n \t 6.1 When HDEV Initialization Fails \tAdded information about Nintendo Target Manager 2.\n Version 2.0 (2019-09-10)\n \tLocation of Changes \tChanges\n \t5. Setting Up NintendoSDK \t Updated the procedure because it is no longer necessary to install SystemUpdater for HDEV when initializing the HDEV in NX Add-On 9.3.0 and later.\n Version 1.0 2019-07-31\n \tInitial version.\nCONFIDENTIAL\n",3,"en-US"],
["Pages/Page_522542771.html","1 Introduction",
"\t\t\n1 Introduction\nThis document describes the procedures to run applications on the HDEV for people who are using a HDEV for the first time.\nUse the following procedures to run applications on HDEV hardware.\nCheck 2 HDEV Operating Environment prior to setup.\n \tChapter\n \t 3 Setting Up the HDEV\n \t 4 Using Nintendo Dev Interface\n \t 5 Setting Up NintendoSDK\n \t 6 Initializing the HDEV\n \t 7 Installing Applications\n \t 8 Running Applications\nCONFIDENTIAL\n",0,"en-US"],
["Pages/Page_522542772.html","2 HDEV Operating Environment",
"\t\t\n2 HDEV Operating Environment\nThe following environment is required for setting up the HDEV and running applications.\n \tOperating system \t \tMicrosoft Windows 10 Pro (64-bit)\n \tMicrosoft Windows 11 Pro (64-bit)\n Note\n An account with administrator rights is required.\n Note\n Behavior is not assured if you change the system locale in the operating system to something other than the default value.\n \t .NET Framework\n \t \tMicrosoft .NET Framework 4.7.2 or later\n Info\n If not already installed, download and install from the following link.\n https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/download/details.aspx?id=49981\n \t.NET Core SDK \t \t.NET Core SDK 3.1.420\n Info\n .NET Core SDK is required for the following versions of NintendoSDK.\n \tVersions of NintendoSDK between 12.2.0 and 14.3.0\n \tVersions of NintendoSDK between 15.0.0 and 15.2.0\n Info\n If not installed, use the installer located in the following path.\n \tNintendoSDK\\Installers\\DotNetSdk\\v3.1\\dotnet-sdk-3.1.420-win-x64.exe\n \t.NET 6 SDK \t \t.NET 6 SDK 6.0.400\n Info\n .NET 6 SDK is required for the following versions of NintendoSDK.\n \tNintendoSDK 15.x.x and later\n \tNintendoSDK 14.x.x versions released after the release of NintendoSDK 15.0.0\n Info\n If not installed, use the installer located in the following path.\n \tNintendoSDK\\Installers\\DotNetSdk\\v6\\dotnet-sdk-6.0.400-win-x64.exe\n \tNX Add-On \t \tNintendoSDK NX Add-On 9.2.0 or later\n \tSystemUpdater \t \tNintendoSDK SystemUpdater for NX 9.0.0-3.0 or later\nCONFIDENTIAL\n",0,"en-US"],
["Pages/Page_522542773.html","3 Setting Up the HDEV",
"\t\t\n3 Setting Up the HDEV\nWe recommend charging the HDEV for about one hour before setup.\nCharge by connecting the AC adapter to the USB Type-C™ terminal on the bottom of the HDEV console.\nCONFIDENTIAL\n",0,"en-US"],
["Pages/Page_522542775.html","4 Using Nintendo Dev Interface",
"\t\t\n4 Using Nintendo Dev Interface\nAbout Nintendo Dev Interface\nNintendo Dev Interface (NDI) is a tool for setting up and managing the development environment.\n Installing NDI\n Info\n You must have Windows administrator rights to install NDI.\n \tProcedure \tOperation\n \t1 \t Download the NDI installer package from the Nintendo Developer Portal.\n \t2 \t Double-click the installer from the downloaded package to run it.\n \t3 \t Select the language for installation.\n \t4 \t Follow the installer instructions to configure the program.\n You can change the installation path from this screen.\n On this screen, add a shortcut to the Start Menu.\n If you select Add application directory to PATH, the path to the NDI program is added to the environment variable.\n This option is useful when calling NDI functions from a command line.\n To start the installation, click Next to proceed.\n Note: NDI requires .NET Framework 4.6.2.\n　 If .NET Framework 4.6.2 is not installed, its installer starts.\n　 Follow the instructions to install .NET Framework 4.6.2.\n \t5 \t To finish the installation, click Finish.\n Note: If you have installed .NET Framework 4.6.2 using the preceding procedure, restart Windows before you start Nintendo Dev Interface and run it from the Start menu.\n A shortcut to the program is added to the Start menu.\nCONFIDENTIAL\n",0,"en-US"],
["Pages/Page_522542790.html","5 Setting Up NintendoSDK",
"\t\t\n5 Setting Up NintendoSDK\nThe NintendoSDK is obtained from the Nintendo server using NDI. NDI downloads the NintendoSDK from the Nintendo server, and then extracts and installs the necessary files.\nInstalling the NintendoSDK Package\n Info\n Windows Administrator privileges are required to use NDI.\n \tProcedure \tOperation\n \t1 \t Start NDI and select Dev Environments > Add Environment > Create My Own > Install to Disk.\n →\n →\n \t2 \t In the Connect to Nintendo Server dialog box, enter the following information.\n \t Server: Select Nintendo Developer Portal.\n \t Nintendo Developer ID: Enter the NDID you use when logging in to the Nintendo Developer Portal.\n Then click the Connect button. When the NDID dialog box is displayed, enter your password to log in.\n Info\n NDI communicates with the Nintendo Developer Portal because the packages it handles are based on Nintendo Developer Portal permissions.\n \t3 \t For the platform, select Nintendo Switch. For the tool set, select SystemUpdater. Then click Next.\n Info\n To install packages other than SystemUpdater and Target Manager, select Standard.\n \t4 \t Select the version of SystemUpdater you want to install and click Next.\nNote: To initialize the HDEV, you must select SystemUpdater version 9.0.0+Build5.0 or later.\n \t5 \t Enter the path where the environment will be created, and the name of the directory (using the environment name). Then click Next.\n Info\n The NintendoSDK will be installed in this directory.\n To install multiple versions or installation package types of the NintendoSDK, you can create multiple directories.\n Info\n False Positives From Antivirus Software\n When installing various NintendoSDK packages, NintendoSDK files can be detected incorrectly as viruses by antivirus software.\nWhen such false positives occur, exclude the NintendoSDK install destination folder from the virus scan, and try installing again.\n \t6 \t The packages that are installed by default are displayed. Confirm the list and click Next.\n \t7 \t Check the type and version of the package to be installed. If there are no issues click Install. Download and installation will begin.\n →\n \t8 \t USB driver installation can be verified during NintendoSDK installation. Install the driver.\n \t9 \t On the screen shown when NintendoSDK installation is completed, click Finish.\n A management screen now appears, listing the actions you can perform in the installed environment.\n Keep this management screen open, because it will be used for later procedures.\nCONFIDENTIAL\n",0,"en-US"],
["Pages/Page_522542818.html","6 Initializing the HDEV",
"\t\t\n6 Initializing the HDEV\nTurning the HDEV On\nPress the POWER Button on the top of the HDEV console.\nPower to the HDEV automatically turns on when connected to a PC when the power is off.\n Note\n The HDEV must be sufficiently charged in advance. We recommend charging for at least an hour.\n If the battery runs low during initialization, the update might fail.\nConnecting the HDEV to a PC\nConnect the USB charging cable to the PC's USB port and the USB Type-C terminal on the bottom of the HDEV console.\n Note\n Do not use the relay box during initialization.\n If the relay box is used during initialization, the update might fail.\n The USB driver might update when the HDEV is connected to a PC.\n When the HDEV is connected to a PC, wait for a while after turning on the HDEV's power.\n If the USB driver is updated during initialization, the update might fail.\nInitializing the HDEV\nInitialize the HDEV firmware using a special tool. (This process usually takes about five minutes.)\n Info\n Warning messages about the Windows Firewall are displayed several times during this process. Select Allow access.\n \tProcedure \tOperation\n \t1 \t Note the code written on the sticker applied to the bottom of the HDEV.\n (This code represents the HDEV's serial number. It is XXX00000000000 in the following example.)\n Info\n The serial number is used to select the HDEV in step 3.\n \t2 \t On the NintendoSDK management screen, click Initialize the HDEV.\n Info\n To display the NintendoSDK management screen, select Dev Environments > Manage from the Nintendo Dev Interface Welcome screen.\n \t3 \t Select the HDEV you want to initialize and begin initialization.\n From the HDEV connection list under 1. Select HDEV actual equipment, click the address noted in step 1 for the serial number information.\n Under 2. Select firmware variation, in the combo box, select the firmware version to use.\n Info\n We strongly recommend selecting the firmware that was initially suggested, without any changes.\n Before selecting + Prod or + ProdWithLog, read NintendoSDK Documents > Guide to Setting Up the Environment > More Information > Firmware That Approximates Retail Hardware > Initialization for PROD MODE.\n To start HDEV initialization, under 3. Start HDEV initialization, click Start initialization.\n Info\n When the HDEV console is in safe mode, a Safe Mode entry appears in the Name column.\n To start initialization, select that entry and click Start initialization.\n Info\n The log pauses for 80 to 90 seconds during this process. Wait for the log to resume.\n The process may have failed if the log does not resume after several minutes.\n Info\n If the HDEV console you want to initialize does not appear on the HDEV connection list even after restarting and clicking Re-Scan, something might be wrong with the HDEV. For more information, see 6.1 When HDEV Initialization Fails.\n \t4 \t If Success is displayed at the lower left of the log window, press the Exit button to close the tool.\n Info\n Fan Operating Noise\n While executing step 3, the cooling fan inside the HDEV starts running.\nThe fan is somewhat noisy, but that does not mean the unit is malfunctioning.\n Info\n For more information about the setup, see NintendoSDK Documents > Guide to Setting Up the Environment > More Information > Other Information About Setting Up the Development Hardware.\n Info\n A sample has been built to check whether the HDEV was initialized correctly.\n Run the sample as follows.\n \tOn the NintendoSDK management screen, click the Run Sample button.\n \tA logo like the following is displayed if the HDEV was initialized correctly and VSI for NX was installed correctly.\n Note: This sample does not exit automatically. Return to the Menu screen with the HOME Button.\nTroubleshooting\nIf HDEV initialization fails or the console you want to initialize is not on the HDEV connection list, see 6.1 When HDEV Initialization Fails and try initializing several more times.\nIf initialization is still unsuccessful, contact Nintendo support.\nHDEV Drivers\nHDEV drivers must be installed for a PC to recognize the HDEV.\nIn most cases, the HDEV device driver is installed automatically when you use NDI to install NintendoSDK. However, automatic installation sometimes fails depending on the environment.\nUse the following procedure to confirm whether HDEV device drivers are installed.\n \tTurn the HDEV on and connect it to the PC with a USB cable.\n \tOpen Device Manager on the PC.\n \tConfirm that NintendoSdkDebugger or NintendoSDK DevKit is shown in Device Manager.\nIf HDEV device drivers are not installed, use the following procedure to install the drivers manually.\n \tFind \\DevkitDriver\\dpinst.exe in the installation directory for NintendoSDK, and run it.\nThe procedure is now complete.\nCONFIDENTIAL\n",0,"en-US"],
["Pages/Page_522542834.html","6.1 When HDEV Initialization Fails",
"\t\t\n6.1 When HDEV Initialization Fails\nFactors such as the HDEV status can cause initialization to fail.\n \tInitialization fails.\n \tThe HDEV console you want to initialize does not appear (or it no longer appears) on the HDEV connection list in InitializeHdevWin.\nIf this happens, verify the following.\nUsing InitializeHdev.exe (or InitializeHdevWin.exe)\nWe recommend directly calling InitializeHdev.exe or InitializeHdevWin.exe.\nThe following files also call these functions directly.\n \t %NINTENDO_SDK_ROOT\\InitializeHdev.bat\nVerifying the Specified Parameters\nIf you are using the InitializeHdev.exe command-line tool, verify that the specified parameters match. (The serial number works with or without a check digit.)\nRepeating Initialization\nSometimes initializing the HDEV several times is successful. Try to initialize again.\nFully Charging the HDEV Console\nTry initialization after fully charging the HDEV console.\nTo charge the device you must attach the AC adapter. Note that simply connecting the USB cable does not charge the console.\nReconnecting the USB Cable and Turning On the Power\nTry initializing the HDEV console again after reconnecting the USB cable and turning the power on.\nIf initialization is particularly unsuccessful, reconnect the USB cable with the following procedure.\n \tDisconnect the USB cable from the HDEV system.\n \tHold down the POWER Button on the upper left of the HDEV console for approximately 12 seconds to turn off the power.\n \tReconnect the USB cable to the HDEV console while the power is off.\n \tThe HDEV turns on automatically when it is connected. Try initialization again.\nIf you are using the device connected to a relay box, try connecting it directly to the PC without using the relay box.\nExiting Target Manager if It Is Running\nIf Target Manager (Nintendo Target Manager or Nintendo Target Manager 2) is running, exit the program and then try to initialize the HDEV.\nSometimes the Target Manager stays in the system tray, so also check there.\nVerifying That the HDEV Console Is Recognized by the Device Manager\nVerify that the HDEV is recognized as a USB device in Device Manager.\nFor more information, see 6 Initializing the HDEV > HDEV Drivers.\nManually Activating Safe Mode\nSometimes initialization is successful after activating safe mode.\n Info\n Safe mode is a mode that runs with minimum features.\n Use safe mode in the following situations.\n \tThe console is not operating properly and must be restored.\n \tHDEV initialization fails.\n \tThe power is on but the system menu does not appear.\n Safe mode can be activated with user operations.\n After safe mode is activated, you may not be able to start in normal mode until the EDEV is initialized, so we recommend using safe mode only when you must restore or initialize.\nEntering Safe Mode\n \tHold down the POWER Button (for approximately 12 seconds) and turn off the HDEV console with the USB cable connected. (The system will restart after the button is held down for 20 seconds, so do not go much longer than 12 seconds.)\n \tPress the buttons on the upper left of the console in the following order.\n\tPress the POWER Button while holding down the volume down button.\n\tWhile holding down the POWER Button, release the volume down button and press the volume up button.\n\tWhile holding down the volume up button, release the POWER Button.\n\tRelease the volume up button.\n \tTo check whether safe mode is running, see Verifying Whether the System Has Entered Safe Mode. If the system did not enter safe mode correctly, restart the process from step 1.\n Info\n For cases where the system enters the standard startup sequence and displays the menu despite attempting to enter safe mode, sometimes adjusting the intervals between button operations will fix the issue. (This is because some operations may not be detected if the intervals between button operations is too fast.)\n As a guide, try using an interval of 0.6 seconds. It is equivalent to 4 beat intervals at 100 beats per minute (bpm). We recommend searching for a video with \"BPM=100\" that you can use as a guide.\nVerifying Whether the System Has Entered Safe Mode\nUse the following procedure to verify whether the system has entered safe mode.\n \tPress the Re-Scan button to display the systems that are in safe mode \"(Safe Mode)\" in the list of connected systems. (However, only do this when using InitializeHdevWin.exe.)\n \t\"NX DevKit Safe Mode\" is displayed for four seconds on the HDEV console screen.\nUsing USB 2.0 When USB 3.0 Connection Fails\nHDEV setup may fail depending on the compatibility with the USB 3.0 controller.\nTry the following.\n \tUpdate the USB 3.0 controller driver to the latest version.\n \tUpdate the USB 3.0 controller firmware to the latest version. (Sometimes this firmware is not publicly available.)\n \tPerform setup by connecting the device to a USB 2.0 port.\nSetup has been confirmed to fail when using a PC with the following USB 3.0 controller.\n \tRenesas Electronics USB 3.0 Host Controller\nContacting Support\nIf initialization is unsuccessful after attempting these procedures, contact Nintendo support.\nWhen contacting support, the process will be smoother if you include the following information.\n \tSerial number of the HDEV unit\n \tSDK version\n \tInitialization log\tFor InitializeHdevWin, copy the log that appears in the log window to a text file and send in the text file.\n\tFor InitializeHdev, execute a log with the --verbose option added and save it to a file.\nCONFIDENTIAL\n",0,"en-US"],
["Pages/Page_522542856.html","7 Installing Applications",
"\t\t\n7 Installing Applications\nInstalling Applications via microSD Card\nYou can install an application by saving its NSP file on a microSD card, and then inserting the card into the development hardware.\n Info\n For more information about creating NSP files, see NintendoSDK Documents > Features > Building > Creating Application Images.\n \tProcedure \tOperation\n \t1 \t Save the NSP file on the microSD card.\n Note\n If the NSP file is larger than 4 GB, the file cannot be saved unless you use a 64 GB or larger microSD card.\nWhen using a microSD card of 32 GB or less, save the file as described in the section following this table.\n \t2 \tInsert the microSD card into the HDEV.\n \t3 \t In DevMenu, in Application, select Install from SD.\n \t4 \t To install an individual application, select the appropriate NSP.\n To install all NSP files, select Install All.\n Info\n For more information, see NintendoSDK Documents > Features > Controlling the Development Hardware > DevMenu.\nSaving a 4 GB or Larger NSP File to a 32 GB or Smaller microSD Card\nPerform the following procedure.\n \tProcedure \tOperation\n \t1 \t Copy the \\Tools\\DivideNsp\\DivideNsp.ps1 and \\Tools\\DivideNsp\\DivideNsp.bat files from the directory where NintendoSDK is installed to the directory in the microSD card where you want to place the NSP file.\n \t2 \tDrag the NSP file you want to save to the DivideNsp.bat file that was copied to the microSD card.\n \t3 \tThis process is complete after a directory with the same name as the NSP file that you want to save is created.\n Info\n \tIf a directory with the same name as the NSP file that you are trying to save already exists, the error message \"\"***.nsp\" cannot be created because a directory with the same name already exists.\" will be displayed.\nIn this case, either delete the applicable files in advance or change the placement location to a different directory and perform the procedure again.\n \tYou can get the same results by using this process to save an NSP file and directory with the same name in a different location than the SD card and then copying that data to any directory on the microSD card.\nCONFIDENTIAL\n",0,"en-US"],
["Pages/Page_522542868.html","8 Running Applications",
"\t\t\n8 Running Applications\nApplications installed on the HDEV console can be started from the DevMenu.\nRunning Installed Applications\n \tProcedure \tOperation\n \t1 \t Turn on the HDEV console.\n A screen similar to the following is displayed when the DevMenu is started.\n If this screen is not displayed, from the menu on the left, select Application.\n A list of applications installed on the HDEV console is displayed.\n \t2 \t Tap the application you want to run.\n The selected application starts.\nCONFIDENTIAL\n",0,"en-US"],
];
